WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.906
In impartial normal-play games, the last player able to move wins and states have no hidden randomness.

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:13.142
A position is winning if at least one legal move leads to a losing position for the opponent.

00:00:13.142 --> 00:00:18.640
A position is losing if every legal move hands a winning position to the opponent.

00:00:18.967 --> 00:00:29.562
Bouton's theorem proves that a multi-pile Nim position is losing if and only if the bitwise XOR sum of all pile sizes equals zero.

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:39.831
From any nonzero XOR sum, you can reduce a pile sharing the highest set bit of the XOR total to force the new XOR sum to zero.

00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:45.047
From a zero XOR sum, every single legal move breaks the balance.

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:54.314
The Sprague-Grundy theorem assigns every impartial game state an equivalent Nim pile size called its Grundy value or nimber.

00:00:54.314 --> 00:01:03.695
A state's Grundy value is the minimum excluded non-negative integer, or mex, among the Grundy values of its reachable next states.

00:01:03.695 --> 00:01:10.767
Any composite game of independent subgames has a total Grundy value equal to the XOR of its parts.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:20.690
When Grundy values depend only on a pile size with bounded subtraction moves, compute the first few hundred values and look for an arithmetic period.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:29.058
Other games collapse immediately via a mirroring strategy where one player copies the opponent's move across a center of symmetry.

00:01:29.058 --> 00:01:32.791
Always print small Grundy tables to spot the pattern fast.
